{"causes":["Grammaticus was influenced by the works of Origen and Eusebius, which shaped his own theological views.","Ammonius Grammaticus was born in Alexandria, Egypt, a city that played a significant role in the development of early Christianity.","Grammaticus's writings were disseminated throughout the Mediterranean world through the trade networks of the ancient Egyptians."],"effects":["Grammaticus's work 'De Interpretatione Hebraicae Scripture' laid the groundwork for later Christian interpreters.","His emphasis on historical context in understanding biblical prophecy influenced early Christian theologians such as Augustine of Hippo.","The Coptic Orthodox Church continues to cite Grammaticus's writings as authoritative to this day, demonstrating the enduring impact of his ideas."],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"origen","sourceName":"Origen","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"ammonius-grammaticus","targetName":"Ammonius Grammaticus","context":"Grammaticus was influenced by the works of Origen and Eusebius, which shaped his own theological views."},{"sourceSlug":"eusebius","sourceName":"Eusebius","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"ammonius-grammaticus","targetName":"Ammonius Grammaticus","context":"Grammaticus was influenced by the works of Origen and Eusebius, which shaped his own theological views."},{"sourceSlug":"alexandria","sourceName":"Alexandria","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"ammonius-grammaticus","targetName":"Ammonius Grammaticus","context":"Ammonius Grammaticus was born in Alexandria, Egypt."},{"sourceSlug":"agostine_hippo","sourceName":"Augustine of Hippo","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"ammonius-grammaticus","targetName":"Ammonius Grammaticus","context":"Grammaticus's emphasis on historical context in understanding biblical prophecy influenced early Christian theologians such as Augustine of Hippo."},{"sourceSlug":"coptic_orthodox_church","sourceName":"Coptic Orthodox Church","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"ammonius-grammaticus","targetName":"Ammonius Grammaticus","context":"The Coptic Orthodox Church continues to cite Grammaticus's writings as authoritative to this day, demonstrating the enduring impact of his ideas."},{"sourceSlug":"christian_theology","sourceName":"Christian Theology","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"ammonius-grammaticus","targetName":"Ammonius Grammaticus","context":"Grammaticus's work 'De Interpretatione Hebraicae Scripture' laid the groundwork for later Christian interpreters."},{"sourceSlug":"biblical_prophecy","sourceName":"Biblical Prophecy","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"ammonius-grammaticus","targetName":"Ammonius Grammaticus","context":"Grammaticus's emphasis on historical context in understanding biblical prophecy influenced early Christian theologians such as Augustine of Hippo."},{"sourceSlug":"early_christianity","sourceName":"Early Christianity","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"ammonius-grammaticus","targetName":"Ammonius Grammaticus","context":"Grammaticus was born in Alexandria, Egypt, a city that played a significant role in the development of early Christianity."},{"sourceSlug":"theology","sourceName":"Theology","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"ammonius-grammaticus","targetName":"Ammonius Grammaticus","context":"Grammaticus's work 'De Interpretatione Hebraicae Scripture' laid the groundwork for later Christian interpreters."}],"places":[{"name":"Alexandria, Egypt","role":"birthplace"},{"name":"Jerusalem","role":"site_of_influence"},{"name":"Constantinople","role":"center_of_dissemination"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: Ammonius Grammaticus","type":"Reference article","slug":"ammonius-grammaticus"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q695923","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ammonius_Grammaticus"],"tags":["Religious Figures","comparative-religion","doctrine-development","cultural-diffusion"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"","summary":"Ammonius Grammaticus (fl. 2nd century CE) was a Greek grammarian and lexicographer working in the Alexandrian scholarly tradition whose surviving work 'Peri Homoion kai Diaphoron Lexeon' ('On Similar and Different Words') is one of the earliest systematic treatments of synonyms and near-synonyms in the Greek language. The work — more commonly known as the 'Ammonius' — catalogues pairs and groups of Greek words that appear to mean the same thing but which Ammonius argues have distinct and differentiable meanings: a project that belongs to the long Alexandrian grammatical tradition of distinguishing correct usage from incorrect, Attic Greek from degraded Koine, and precise philosophical language from imprecise popular speech.

The 'Peri Homoion' is a lexicographical work of the type that would be called a 'differentiae' in the Latin tradition — a systematic attempt to distinguish among apparently synonymous terms. Its importance lies less in individual entries than in its contribution to the Alexandrian grammatical enterprise of systematic linguistic analysis that fed into Byzantine scholarship and ultimately into the Renaissance recovery of Greek: the tradition of careful word-distinction that Ammonius exemplifies influenced Byzantine educational grammars and the humanist scholarship of the 15th–16th centuries that drew on Byzantine collections.

Ammonius should be distinguished from several other classical figures named Ammonius: Ammonius of Athens (a Platonist philosopher, c. 50–120 CE), Ammonius Saccas (founder of Neoplatonism, c. 175–242 CE), and the later Ammonius son of Hermias (Neoplatonist commentator on Aristotle, c. 440–520 CE). The grammarian's precise dates are uncertain, placing him tentatively in the 2nd–3rd century CE Alexandrian intellectual milieu.","subjects":["Classical Scholarship","Greek Language","Linguistics","Alexandria","Classical Era","Intellectual History","Grammar","Lexicography"],"frameworks":["CULTURAL_TRANSMISSION","STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"Ammonius Grammaticus's work 'De Interpretatione Hebraicae Scripture' laid the groundwork for later Christian interpreters, demonstrating the enduring impact of his ideas on Christian theology.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
